CNML Specification (Draft)

1. Overview

CNML (Cognitive Narrative Markup Language) is a semantic markup language for representing narrative structure, modality, and expressive qualities such as prosody and voice. It is designed to separate:

narrative structure

modality (actual vs nonactual experience)

communicative channels (speech, thought, gesture, etc.)

prosody (how something is said)

voice (baseline speaker characteristics)


CNML prioritizes:

composability

orthogonality of expressive dimensions

renderer independence



---

2. Core Structural Model

2.1 Modal System

CNML uses explicit modal containers:

<narrative>: default, actualized narration

<nonactual type="memory|dream|imagination|hypothetical">: non-actual experiences


Rules:

nonactual may nest within nonactual

think may appear inside nonactual

say may appear inside nonactual

think cannot appear inside say (strict mode)

nonactual cannot appear inside say (strict mode)



---

2.2 Communicative Channels

<say>: spoken utterance

<think>: cognition

<gesture>: bodily movement (non-facial)

<express>: facial/nonverbal expression

<feel>: instinctual/internal affective impulses



---

3. Prosody System

3.1 Design Principles

Prosody tags are orthogonal

Prosody tags compose

Prosody tags define deltas, not absolute values

Prosody is evaluated per text span, not per tree order



---

3.2 Supported Tags

<loud> / <quiet> (amplitude)

<emph> / <deemph> (salience)

<harsh> / <gentle> (texture)

<calm> / <agitated> (arousal)



---

3.3 Evaluation Model

Step 1: Flatten to segments

Example:

<loud>one <emph>two</emph> three</loud>

Becomes:

"one" → {loud}

"two" → {loud, emph}

"three" → {loud}



---

Step 2: Evaluate tag sets

Each segment is evaluated independently:

state = base_voice
for tag in active_tags:
    state += tag.delta

Order does not matter.


---

3.4 Parameter Space

Canonical parameters:

pitch_base

volume

tempo

energy

attack

intonation

timbre (set)



---

4. Voice System

4.1 Definition

Voices are defined globally and referenced by ID.

Example:

<voice id="Diane">
  <dialect>US/California</dialect>
  <accent>transatlantic</accent>
  <range>alto</range>
  <quality>nasal</quality>
  <quality>exaggerated-vowels</quality>
  <style>repressed</style>
</voice>


---

4.2 Semantics

<dialect>: linguistic system

<accent>: phonological realization

<range>: pitch band

<quality>: acoustic traits

<style>: baseline expressive tendency


Voice defines baseline state, not momentary expression.


---

5. Chat / Messaging Layer

<chat>
  <out>
    <from>Me</from>
    <msg>Hello</msg>
  </out>
  <in>
    <from>You</from>
    <msg>Hi</msg>
  </in>
</chat>

Rules:

Full CNML allowed inside <msg>

<from> identifies speaker



---

6. Referential Tags

<work medium="...">: creative works

<quote of="speech|thought">: quoted content


Purpose:

defer formatting decisions



---

7. Profiles

7.1 Strict Mode

enforces nesting constraints

prohibits ambiguous structures

suitable for tooling and validation


7.2 Loose Mode

allows author discretion

renderer interprets intent



---

8. Relax NG Schema (Compact Syntax)

default namespace = ""

element cnml {
  element book {
    element publication { text* },
    element frontmatter { text* }?,
    element mainmatter { text* }
  }
}

# Prosody

element loud { mixed { any* } }
element quiet { mixed { any* } }
element emph { mixed { any* } }
element deemph { mixed { any* } }
element harsh { mixed { any* } }
element gentle { mixed { any* } }
element calm { mixed { any* } }
element agitated { mixed { any* } }

# Communication

element say { mixed { any* } }
element think { mixed { any* } }
element feel { mixed { any* } }
element gesture { mixed { any* } }
element express { mixed { any* } }

# Modal

element nonactual {
  attribute type { "memory" | "dream" | "imagination" | "hypothetical" },
  mixed { any* }
}

# Voice

element voice {
  attribute id { text },
  element dialect { text }?,
  element accent { text }?,
  element range { text }?,
  element quality { text }*,
  element style { text }*
}

# Chat

element chat {
  (element in { chat_block } | element out { chat_block })+
}

chat_block =
  element from { text } &
  element msg { mixed { any* }+ }

# References

element work {
  attribute medium { text },
  text
}

element quote {
  attribute of { text }?,
  text
}

# Generic fallback
any = element * { (attribute * { text } | text | any)* }


---

9. Execution Model Summary

1. Parse XML → tree


2. Flatten into text segments with active tag sets


3. Compute voice state per segment


4. Map to renderer / TTS engine




---

10. Design Philosophy

Structure defines scope

Tags define meaning

Evaluation is order-independent

Rendering is deferred



